From ebe15d1afdb9fd1c6066caba2a4dfa4eb34c40b2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yichao Yu <yyc1992@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 17:35:27 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] slsqp: skip the QR of the triangular LSQ matrix without
 constraints

Without inequality constraints, LSEI solves its least-squares problem
with DGELSY, and without equality constraints the matrix it passes is
the upper triangular LSQ matrix built by LSQ.  Its QR factorization
leaves it unchanged (every reflector is the identity), but LAPACK's
blocked DGEQRF still multiplies the zeros: O(n^3) per iteration, 40% of
the run time on a dense unconstrained problem with n = 800.

lsq_triangular does the remaining steps of DGELSY directly: the same
incremental condition estimate (DLAIC1) for the rank and the same DTRSM
solve, so the result is bit-identical.  DGELSY is still called whenever
it would do something else (rescaling of A or b, or rank deficiency for
the minimum-norm solution).  Triangularity follows from the problem
structure (no equality constraints); debug builds assert it.

On a dense unconstrained quadratic with n = 800 this cuts the time per
evaluation with OpenBLAS from 8.5 ms to 1.7 ms; all evaluated points are
bit-identical.  DLAIC1 is now also taken from the BLAS/LAPACK library,
and the built-in DLAIC1 and DTRSM gain the Fortran calling convention
and the left-side solve needed for this.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
